-- title:
--   Path displacements of ancestor maps
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. An ordered tree has finite levels $0,1,2,\dots$ with a single root, and monotone surjective parent maps from level $k+1$ to level $k$; edge weights are positive and sum to $1$ over the children of each parent. Each node carries a nonexpansive selfmap of $K$, and the tree is evaluated bottom-up: a node's value is its map applied to the weighted average of its children's values. Fix a tree evaluation and a comparison point $z\in K$.
--
--   1. **Path displacement.** $\Delta_0(v,z)=0$ and
--   $$
--   \Delta_{k+1}(v,z)=\Delta_k(\mathrm{par}\,v,z)+\|f_{k,\mathrm{par}\,v}(z)-z\|,
--   $$
--   the total displacement of the proper ancestors of $v$ at $z$.
--   2. **Weighted path displacement.** The same sum with each ancestor's displacement multiplied by its root path weight $\pi$.
--
--   These bound how far the root output can move when one node's input changes. The other children of each ancestor contribute at most their total weight, because $\operatorname{diam}K\le1$.
